 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general municipal law, in relation to allowing alcoholic\nbeverages as prizes in any game of chance",
    "basePrintNo": "S3009",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3009-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 61,
       "fullName": "Michael H. Ranzenhofer",
       "imgName": "406_michael_h._ranzenhofer.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 406,
       "sessionMemberId": 1285,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "RANZENHOFER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§195-d & 190-a, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3009\n\nSPONSOR: FUNKE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general municipal law, in relation to allowing alco-\nholic beverages as prizes in any game of chance\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to allow bona fide charitable organizations\nparticipating in games of chance such as raffles, etc., to offer alco-\nholic beverages as a prize.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSections 1: Strikes the last line of section 195-d of the general munic-\nipal law that states \"No alcoholic beverage shall be offered or given as\na prize in any game of chance\".\n\nSection 2: Creates restrictions for raffles that award prizes containing\nalcohol.\n\nSection 3: Sets the effective date immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation looks to cut unnecessary, over burdensome and outdated\nbureaucracy around prizes offered for charitable gaming, while promoting\ncharitable donations and helping nonprofits with their fundraising\nactivities.\n\nCurrently, New York State Law prohibits charitable organizations partic-\nipating in a game of chance to raffle off prizes that contain alcohol.\nThough State Law outlaws this action, most charitable gaming organiza-\ntions do not know this is illegal and partake in the activity as a form\nof common practice. It is common for a local winery, brewery, or bever-\nage distributor to donate a basket of their products to a charitable\norganization to help raise money for the cause the charitable organiza-\ntion supports.\n\nThe prohibition on alcohol as a prize came about in 1976, when the laws\naround charitable gaming first came into existence due to a constitu-\ntional amendment that the voters approved in the 1975 general election\n(Article I, S9) - which permits localities to authorize, subject to\nstate legislative supervision and control, the conduce of games of\nchance events.\n\nAccording to the 10-day budget report of this legislation in 1976, the\nvoter referendum on games of chance came about because games of chance\noperations were being conducted by charitable and service organizations\non a widespread and open basis, although such games were illegal.\n\nThis legislation looks to make changes to the laws of 1976 for similar\nreasons, with raffling off alcohol as a common, widespread practice\nalthough such activity is illegal. This new legislation will clarify\nconfusion within the beverage industry and charitable gaming groups,\nwhile accelerating the growth of craft beverages, beer, wine and spirits\nindustries and bona fide charitable and service organizations. Craft\nbreweries, wineries and distilleries often donate products to charities\nas in-kind donations, as a way to help the charity while at the same\ntime promoting their products. We should not be restricting the growth\nof one of New York's fastest growing industries. Wineries, cideries and\nbrewery businesses in New York State have showed consistent and signif-\nicant growth over the last few years and are putting themselves at\nlegal-risk when they donate their products to charities as in-kind\ndonations for raffle purposes.\n\nAccording to the New York State Brewers Association, the number of New\nYork State breweries grew from 95 in 2012 to 207 in 2014, making them\n3rd in the country with an Economic Impact of $3.5 Billion dollars.  In\n2012, New York State was the 6th largest beer producing state behind\nCalifornia, Pennsylvania, Colorado, Ohio and Oregon. New York State\ncraft breweries increased production by 26% from 557,436 in 2011 to\n859,536 barrels in 2012, while at the same time nationally, the craft\nbeer segment only grew by 15%. According to the New York Wine and Grape\nFoundation, the New York grape, grape juice and wine industry generates\nmore than $4.8 billion in economic benefits annually for New York State.\nNew York is home to over 1,600 family vineyards and over 400 wineries,\nproducing more than 175,000,000 bottles of wine per year while providing\n$408 million in state and local taxes.\n\nWhile the local beverage industry is growing, charitable gaming revenue\nhas remained stagnant. Groups that participate in charitable gaming are\nauthorized organizations, defined as any bona fide religious, charita-\nble, educational, service, veteran or volunteer fire organization which\noperates without profit to its members. These organizations foster and\nsupport worthy causes and undertakings that the majority of the elector-\nate support. The raffles they host are one of the many fundraising tech-\nniques the organizations use. Raffling off donated products helps\npromote the product itself, while providing the charitable organization\none-hundred percent profit - a win for both the charity and business.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.6265/A.8623 of 2016; Passed Senate\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
